# ============================================================================
# FlowElasticSolid / 2D perforation of a porous aluminium plate by a titanium cylinder
# Favrie & Schmidmayer, CMAME 455 (2026) 118880, Sec. 5.4, Fig. 13 (results at 1, 3, 5, 10, 20 microseconds)
#
# Units: GPa, g/cm^3, cm, km/s -> time unit 10 microseconds.  Domain 2.4 cm x 2.4 cm, 400 x 400 cells (the paper's
# grid for Sec. 5.3), outflow on all sides.  Cylinder of diameter 0.2 cm centred at (0.5, 1.2) moving at 700 m/s in +x;
# plate 0.6 < x < 0.8, 0.4 < y < 2.0 with 40 % of air in its pores; p = 1e5 Pa.
# Three materials in the two-phase solver: phase 0 = the solid with species Al and Ti (species stage 2b), phase 1 = air.
# GTN yield function + compaction energy for both metals (elastic.compaction), instantaneous pressure relaxation.
# ASSUMPTIONS as in input_1D_TiSpallation: associated flow rule, 1/rho_r in the compaction energy, GTN viscosity
# read as table value / sigma_y (Al 5.4e9 / 60e6 = 90 Pa s, Ti 4.2135e8 / 530e6 = 0.795 Pa s).
# The paper runs this one on 100 x 100 base cells with one refinement level (200 x 200 effective); 400 x 400 here.
# The solid trace in the air (1e-4) is labelled titanium: aluminium is the plate only, so the cylinder has no
# aluminium rim in the plots.
# The paper shows no numbers for this test, only fields (its Fig. 13); the comparison is by figure.
#
# Run (from bin/): mpirun -np 16 ./hydro2-2d-g++ ../tests/FlowElasticSolid/input_2D_TiCylinder_PorousAl amr.max_grid_size=100 plot_file=<dir>/output
# Plot:            python3 ../tests/FlowElasticSolid/reference/favrie_schmidmayer_2d_plots.py perforation <dir>
# ============================================================================
